Package: mica-busybox
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: emergency BusyBox binary, unexpanded
 One file: /usr/bin/busybox, Debian's own binary for this architecture, taken
 out of the busybox archive of the pinned trixie base without installing it.
 An operator whose normal userland is damaged reaches an applet by NAMING it --
 `busybox sh`, `busybox ls`, `busybox mount` -- and that is the whole interface
 this package offers.
 .
 NO APPLET LINKS ARE SHIPPED AND NONE IS GENERATED. Not in /usr/bin, not in a
 directory of their own, not at any point in PATH. Two hundred and seventy
 applet names in PATH would silently re-decide what `ls`, `tar`, `mount` and
 `sh` mean for every script in this image and in the verifier, and the semantics
 differ from GNU's in ways a passing build does not see. The GNU tools this
 image ships stay the command set; this binary is a diagnostic tool beside
 them, and `verify/`'s busybox family asserts exactly that against the packed
 root.
 .
 Debian's own busybox package is NOT depended on and NOT installed, and the
 difference is not packaging taste. That package ships
 /usr/share/initramfs-tools/hooks/zz-busybox and a conf-hooks.d fragment which
 together copy busybox into the initramfs AND build a hard-link farm of every
 applet inside it. Both are precisely what this package exists not to do, and
 on x64 the kernel package's postinst would have run the hook during the
 compose. Unpacking the archive and keeping one file is what makes the image's
 early boot independent of this binary rather than merely believed to be.
 .
 NOTHING MAY DEPEND ON THIS PACKAGE. No unit names it, no initramfs hook, no
 generator, no script in the overlay -- a fallback that a normal service comes
 to rely on is no longer an emergency tool but part of the boot contract, and
 it would be one nobody tested. This package is a leaf on purpose.
 .
 It is dynamically linked, which is Debian's build and is deliberate: it links
 the same libc the rest of the image does, so it is one binary rather than a
 second C library in the root. The cost is stated rather than papered over --
 a system damaged badly enough to lose /lib is damaged badly enough to lose
 this too. This package is not a rescue environment and does not promise one;
 that is a separate design.
